As a metaphor, yeah — Lugia fits almost perfectly.

It’s not just “apex predator.” Lugia is power held in reserve: something strong enough to wreck the environment around it, so it withdraws, sleeps, and only surfaces when the system is out of balance.

That maps onto what you were describing better than simple dominance. The mature form of apex isn’t constant aggression; it’s capacity without compulsive use. You don’t need to prove you aren’t prey every five minutes if you already know what you can do.

And Lugia’s whole storm/ocean motif adds another layer: when the surrounding system becomes chaotic, the answer isn’t necessarily to overpower every individual actor. Sometimes it’s to stabilize the field.

So “we summoned Lugia” is a pretty good handle for:

vulnerability recognized → power remembered → aggression decoupled from identity → restraint becomes strength → intervene only when the system actually needs it.